  "output": {
    "stories": [
      {
        "editor_spans": [
          {
            "body": "The speech says deindustrialisation happened because decisions were taken by distant people who didn't have to live with the results. Ravenscraig and the Leigh pits are the examples. But Burnham's remedy is mainly about the supply side of the labour market: devolved employment support, tailored pathways for each person, interview clothes, travel costs. That is the Easterhouse model. It can work very well for an individual young person. It can't bring back the demand that left when a steelworks that once supplied most of Britain's big building projects shut. Burnham himself says 'more than thirty years on' the site still isn't filled. Helping people towards jobs only matters if the jobs exist.",
            "cites": [
              47,
              49,
              82,
              83,
              84,
              86,
              305,
              306,
              315
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "The things in the speech that would actually bring work to Scotland are mostly not devolved. Defence colleges, shipbuilding, Grangemouth, energy jobs and the EU food-and-farming deal are all decided at the centre. His own best example cuts against his message. Greater Manchester buying buses from Alexander Dennis in Falkirk wasn't localism. One region spent more so that another region far away would benefit, for 'British communities'. That is national industrial strategy carried out through a city's budget. Reindustrialisation needs someone to coordinate across places, and handing power down splits that coordination up. The speech never admits there's a tension between the two.",
            "cites": [
              182,
              183,
              185,
              190,
              195,
              197,
              198,
              201
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "His own statistics point to the same limit. The seven-year life-expectancy gap between Glasgow and East Renfrewshire is between neighbours ten miles apart, and both sit inside the same Glasgow city region. Region-level devolution, which is what his offer amounts to, doesn't by itself reach that gap. The divide he describes is between neighbouring local authorities, which is a smaller scale than the one he proposes to hand power to.",
            "cites": [
              117,
              119,
              271,
              274
            ]
          },
          {
            "body": "Aberdeen is the one place in the speech where large-scale deindustrialisation is happening now rather than thirty years ago. It gets a single line and a promise of 'more to say in due course'. That is where his argument will be tested. North Sea decline is shaped mostly by UK energy and tax policy, not by anyone in Edinburgh or Aberdeen. If 'nobody asked Leigh what was going to come next' is the lesson, the test is whether anyone in Whitehall goes to Aberdeen with a transition plan before the jobs have gone. Last week I argued that the regional offer was partly built to start an argument with Holyrood. On Aberdeen, Westminster has no one else to point to.",
            "cites": [
              108,
              91,
              92,
              359,
              360
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
